Functional Requirements:
User can send a message to another user
User can receive messages in the same order they were sent
Messages should be delivered when the receiver comes online
Sender should know the delivery status of a message (sent / delivered / read)

Non-Functional Requirements:
Low latency message delivery (near real-time)
High availability (messages should work even during failures)
Reliability & durability (messages should not be lost)
Scalability to support millions of concurrent users
Security (end-to-end encryption)

Typical components:
Client (mobile app) , Load Balancer , Chat / Messaging Server , Presence Service , Message Queue , Storage

📤 Message Send Flow (Correct Order)
User A sends message from client
Client sends message to Chat Server (via persistent connection)
Chat Server:
Assigns message ID
Pushes message to Message Queue
Message is stored in Message Store (durability). Chat Server checks Presence Service: If User B is online → deliver immediately . If offline → keep in store + send push notification

“When a user sends a message, the client sends it to the Chat Server over a persistent connection. The Chat Server assigns a message ID, pushes the message to a queue, and persists it in the Message Store to 
ensure durability.  The server then checks the receiver’s presence—if the receiver is online, the message is delivered immediately; otherwise, it is stored and delivered when the receiver comes online.”

“WhatsApp ensures reliable and ordered delivery by persisting messages before delivery, assigning per-conversation sequence numbers to maintain ordering,
and using acknowledgements with retries to guarantee exactly-once delivery.”

“WebSockets are preferred over HTTP because they provide a persistent, bidirectional connection that enables real-time message delivery with low latency. HTTP polling is inefficient, increases latency, 
and wastes server resources due to repeated requests.”

“When a message is sent, the Chat Server assigns a message ID and stores it in the Message Store to ensure durability. If the receiver is offline, the message is marked as pending and a push notification is sent. When 
the receiver comes online and reconnects via WebSocket, the server delivers all pending messages and waits for acknowledgements.”

“Push notifications are used to notify and wake up offline users that new messages are available, but actual message delivery happens only after the client reconnects via WebSocket.”

Scaling Whatsapp
“WhatsApp scales by distributing users across multiple chat servers using persistent WebSocket connections. Message queues are used to handle high traffic without overloading servers, and messages are stored durably 
to support offline users. Push notifications act as a wake-up signal, and failures are handled through retries and reconnections, allowing the system to scale to millions of users reliably.”

“Queues in WhatsApp are used to buffer messages and decouple message sending from delivery, so the system can handle high traffic reliably without overloading chat servers.”
Queue ≠ storage replacement
Queue = traffic smoother + reliability layer

“Messaging systems are write-heavy and require horizontal scaling. NoSQL databases allow partitioning data by chatId and 
provide fast writes without complex joins.”“For a one-to-one conversation, a unique chatId is created and reused for all messages between the two users. Messages are stored in DynamoDB with chatId as the partition key and a monotonically 
increasing sequence number as the sort key, which allows efficient ordering and pagination of messages.”

“A hot partition occurs when a single partition key receives a very high volume of requests, causing throttling. In chat systems, this happens for very active chats. We solve it by splitting the partition using time-based buckets, not
by removing the chatId.”


